## What changed

**[js/auth.js](js/auth.js)** — added a `safeRedirect` helper at module level and exposed it as `Auth.safeRedirect` so page scripts can use it:

- Whitelist: `dashboard.html`, `problem.html`, `problem-list.html`, `evidence.html`, `profile.html`, `skill.html` — plus an optional query string (e.g. `problem.html?id=10`).
- Anything else falls back to `dashboard.html`: absolute URLs, `javascript:`/`data:` URIs, protocol-relative `//host`, unknown pages, `../` escapes, malformed percent-encoding, and any smuggling characters (whitespace/control chars, quotes, backslashes).
- Single-decodes percent-encoded input (`problem.html%3Fid%3D10` → `problem.html?id=10`), so double-encoded payloads like `problem.html%253F...` fail the exact whitelist match and are rejected.
- `Auth.requireAuth` now sanitizes its outgoing `?redirect=` value before encoding it into the login URL.
- [signup.html](signup.html) checked: it never reads a `redirect` param — its three redirects are hard-coded constants — so no change was needed there.

**[login.html](login.html)** — both spots (the already-authenticated early redirect, and the post-login redirect) now call `window.Auth.safeRedirect(redirect)` instead of the raw `decodeURIComponent(redirect)` assignment.

